WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.448 --> 00:00:02.293
Claude Fable 5.2 is out.

00:00:02.790 --> 00:00:06.689
It's our most capable generally
available model for technical fields.

00:00:07.432 --> 00:00:10.546
With several models to choose
from, how do you pick the right

00:00:10.546 --> 00:00:12.014
one for the task in front of you?

00:00:12.054 --> 00:00:15.195
Most of the time, the answer is Opus 5.5

00:00:15.195 --> 00:00:16.095
on medium effort.

00:00:16.464 --> 00:00:19.917
It's the daily driver for complex
coding and knowledge work, and

00:00:19.918 --> 00:00:21.757
the starting point we recommend.

00:00:22.057 --> 00:00:25.621
As an example, I am working on
a online shop and I would like

00:00:25.622 --> 00:00:27.033
to add monthly subscriptions.

00:00:27.633 --> 00:00:31.157
That change touches the data
model, the API, and the UI.

00:00:31.757 --> 00:00:35.413
I'll leave the model and effort level
on the default and start the task.

00:00:36.001 --> 00:00:39.969
If the result falls short, the first
thing to try is raising the effort level.

00:00:40.548 --> 00:00:43.275
It's a smaller step than
changing the model, and it

00:00:43.275 --> 00:00:44.921
usually keeps the prompt cache.

00:00:45.639 --> 00:00:48.583
Some work, however, is more
ambitious than a single feature.

00:00:49.183 --> 00:00:51.881
I'd like to migrate the whole
shop to a new architecture.

00:00:52.380 --> 00:00:56.783
It's a large, loosely defined problem
across the whole codebase, with

00:00:56.826 --> 00:01:00.411
dozens of milestones and decisions
that nobody has written down yet.

00:01:00.991 --> 00:01:05.435
For work that is ambitious and
open-ended, where judgment and accuracy

00:01:05.441 --> 00:01:10.759
matter, move to Fable. At twice the
price of Opus per token, it's suited

00:01:10.759 --> 00:01:13.142
to work where quality is paramount.

00:01:13.629 --> 00:01:16.302
I'll give Fable the goal
and two non-negotiables.

00:01:16.741 --> 00:01:20.337
Checkout can't go down
and orders can't be lost.

00:01:20.879 --> 00:01:24.704
It makes the plan, does the
work, and tests as it goes, so

00:01:24.705 --> 00:01:28.044
the result is much closer to what
I consider done when I review it.

00:01:28.644 --> 00:01:32.379
In the other direction, for
well-scoped work where speed and

00:01:32.382 --> 00:01:36.297
cost matters, consider moving
down to a faster, cheaper model.

00:01:36.897 --> 00:01:41.860
Let's say a bug report comes in for
the shop with a clear reproduction.

00:01:42.658 --> 00:01:45.194
I can say what done looks
like in one sentence.

00:01:45.736 --> 00:01:50.112
Sonnet is suited to well-scoped tasks
like this one, and it's faster than

00:01:50.112 --> 00:01:52.273
Opus at half the price per token.

00:01:52.939 --> 00:01:56.578
For even simpler work, you can
move further down to Haiku.

00:01:57.038 --> 00:02:00.426
It's the fastest and cheapest
of the family, and it's well

00:02:00.426 --> 00:02:04.634
suited to high-volume, cost-sensitive
work like summarization,

00:02:04.636 --> 00:02:06.616
classification, and routing.

00:02:07.216 --> 00:02:12.050
For example, the shop has an inbox for
customer messages, and I can add a feature

00:02:12.053 --> 00:02:14.237
that sorts each one as it comes in.

00:02:14.837 --> 00:02:19.154
That feature runs on Haiku through
the Claude API many times a day.

00:02:19.795 --> 00:02:22.437
You can also split one
job across many models.

00:02:22.808 --> 00:02:26.673
Let's say the shop has to move
to a new major version of its

00:02:26.773 --> 00:02:28.953
framework across every package.

00:02:29.366 --> 00:02:34.896
It's a large, complex migration,
so I'll use Fable, but not every

00:02:34.897 --> 00:02:37.105
part of it needs its capabilities.

00:02:37.385 --> 00:02:41.779
Upgrading each package and fixing
its tests is a well-scoped task.

00:02:42.379 --> 00:02:46.753
In Claude Code, the main session can
hand parts like these to subagents.

00:02:47.233 --> 00:02:50.583
A subagent is a separate
Claude with its own context.

00:02:50.997 --> 00:02:53.949
It does one part of the
job and reports back.

00:02:54.452 --> 00:02:58.528
So I've asked Fable to lead the
migration and hand those parts to Sonnet.

00:02:59.028 --> 00:03:02.523
Alright, hopefully that gives you
a sense of where each model fits.

00:03:02.762 --> 00:03:07.016
Most tasks start on Opus, the
daily driver for complex coding.

00:03:07.616 --> 00:03:11.006
The most ambitious, open-ended
work goes to Fable.

00:03:11.606 --> 00:03:15.902
Well-scoped work where speed
or cost matters goes to Sonnet.

00:03:16.502 --> 00:03:20.225
And high-volume, cost-sensitive
work goes to Haiku.

00:03:20.838 --> 00:03:25.504
To measure how each model does on your
own tasks and projects, follow the

00:03:25.504 --> 00:03:27.326
steps in the guide linked below.

